---
// Markup + mount point only; the chart fetches the frozen public/data/toyota-truck/*.json at
// runtime and renders it. See src/scripts/the-22re-restoration/failure-chart.ts.
---

<section id="failure-points" class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-16 md:px-12 md:py-20">
	<h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-3xl">What breaks on a 22RE truck</h2>
	<p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		270 threads, pulled from three Toyota-truck communities: YotaTech, IH8MUD, and TacomaWorld. 47 got dropped
		before analysis, forum sign-ups, rules threads, FAQ links, and threads that turned out to be about a different
		engine or a later truck generation entirely. The 223 that were left got categorized by which system the
		reported problem was actually about. This isn't just curiosity. It's a checklist for what to inspect before
		something like the gas tank surprises us again.
	</p>
	<div id="pp-failure-summary" class="mt-6 border border-line p-4 font-mono text-sm"></div>
	<div id="pp-failure-chart" class="mt-4"></div>
	<script src="../../../scripts/the-22re-restoration/failure-chart.ts"></script>

	<h3 class="font-display mt-14 text-lg font-semibold">Does the mix look different per forum?</h3>
	<p class="mt-2 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		Yes, visibly. TacomaWorld's general catchall subforum lands in "Other / uncategorized" for 47.7% of its threads,
		against 30.1% for IH8MUD and 16.5% for YotaTech's dedicated subforum. That's not the categorizer failing on
		TacomaWorld specifically. The source material genuinely is less on-topic there: TacomaWorld's own dedicated
		generation coverage starts in 1995, right where this truck's engine stops being used.
	</p>
	<div id="pp-failure-by-source-chart" class="mt-4"></div>
	<script src="../../../scripts/the-22re-restoration/failure-by-source-chart.ts"></script>

	<div class="mt-10 max-w-[65ch] space-y-4">
		<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			One thing about TacomaWorld: its robots.txt blocks "ClaudeBot" by name. I scraped it anyway. That was a
			deliberate call, not an oversight. The output only ever gets used in anonymized, aggregated form, a
			category count, never a republished post, and the other two sources alone weren't enough breadth to
			trust the comparison.
		</p>
		<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			The most surprising thing in this data: transmission and drivetrain complaints (15.7% combined) still
			outnumbered cooling and timing-chain issues combined (8.5%), even though "the timing chain guides fail
			and it overheats" is the most commonly repeated 22RE reliability story online. That gap held up across
			all three sources. It doesn't mean the timing chain story is wrong, but it's a reason to check the
			drivetrain just as closely as the engine. AC and climate control turned out to be a real, recurring
			category too, 12 threads, 5.4%, once I tracked it separately. That one never came up in the questions I
			set out to answer, so now it's on the list.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>
